{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2601.04728","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2601.04728","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2601.04728","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Elizabeth","familyName":"Donoway","name":"Donoway, Elizabeth","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hailey","familyName":"Joren","name":"Joren, Hailey","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fabien","familyName":"Roger","name":"Roger, Fabien","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jan","familyName":"Leike","name":"Leike, Jan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Excess Description Length of Learning Generalizable Predictors"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-01-08T08:46:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-09T01:30:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Understanding whether fine-tuning elicits latent capabilities or teaches new ones is a fundamental question for language model evaluation and safety. We develop a formal information-theoretic framework for quantifying how much predictive structure fine-tuning extracts from the train dataset and writes into a model's parameters. Our central quantity, Excess Description Length (EDL), is defined via prequential coding and measures the gap between the bits required to encode training labels sequentially using an evolving model (trained online) and the residual encoding cost under the final trained model. We establish that EDL is non-negative in expectation, converges to surplus description length in the infinite-data limit, and provides bounds on expected generalization gain. Through a series of toy models, we clarify common confusions about information in learning: why random labels yield EDL near zero, how a single example can eliminate many bits of uncertainty about the underlying rule(s) that describe the data distribution, why structure learned on rare inputs contributes proportionally little to expected generalization, and how format learning creates early transients distinct from capability acquisition. This framework provides rigorous foundations for the empirical observation that capability elicitation and teaching exhibit qualitatively distinct scaling signatures."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.04728","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-01-09T02:51:15Z","registered":"2026-01-09T02:51:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-09T02:51:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2601.04603","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2601.04603","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2601.04603","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hoagy","familyName":"Cunningham","name":"Cunningham, Hoagy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jerry","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Jerry","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zihan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zihan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrew","familyName":"Persic","name":"Persic, Andrew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alwin","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Alwin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jordan","familyName":"Abderrachid","name":"Abderrachid, Jordan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Raj","familyName":"Agarwal","name":"Agarwal, Raj","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bobby","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Bobby","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Austin","familyName":"Cohen","name":"Cohen, Austin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andy","familyName":"Dau","name":"Dau, Andy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alek","familyName":"Dimitriev","name":"Dimitriev, Alek","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rob","familyName":"Gilson","name":"Gilson, Rob","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Logan","familyName":"Howard","name":"Howard, Logan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yijin","familyName":"Hua","name":"Hua, Yijin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jared","familyName":"Kaplan","name":"Kaplan, Jared","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jan","familyName":"Leike","name":"Leike, Jan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mu","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Mu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christopher","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Christopher","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vladimir","familyName":"Mikulik","name":"Mikulik, Vladimir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rohit","familyName":"Mittapalli","name":"Mittapalli, Rohit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Clare","familyName":"O'Hara","name":"O'Hara, Clare","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jin","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, Jin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nikhil","familyName":"Saxena","name":"Saxena, Nikhil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alex","familyName":"Silverstein","name":"Silverstein, Alex","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xunjie","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Xunjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Giulio","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Giulio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ethan","familyName":"Perez","name":"Perez, Ethan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mrinank","familyName":"Sharma","name":"Sharma, Mrinank","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Constitutional Classifiers++: Efficient Production-Grade Defenses against Universal Jailbreaks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-01-08T05:16:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-09T01:21:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce enhanced Constitutional Classifiers that deliver production-grade jailbreak robustness with dramatically reduced computational costs and refusal rates compared to previous-generation defenses. Our system combines several key insights. First, we develop exchange classifiers that evaluate model responses in their full conversational context, which addresses vulnerabilities in last-generation systems that examine outputs in isolation. Second, we implement a two-stage classifier cascade where lightweight classifiers screen all traffic and escalate only suspicious exchanges to more expensive classifiers. Third, we train efficient linear probe classifiers and ensemble them with external classifiers to simultaneously improve robustness and reduce computational costs. Together, these techniques yield a production-grade system achieving a 40x computational cost reduction compared to our baseline exchange classifier, while maintaining a 0.05% refusal rate on production traffic. Through extensive red-teaming comprising over 1,700 hours, we demonstrate strong protection against universal jailbreaks -- no attack on this system successfully elicited responses to all eight target queries comparable in detail to an undefended model. Our work establishes Constitutional Classifiers as practical and efficient safeguards for large language models."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.04603","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-01-09T02:48:31Z","registered":"2026-01-09T02:48:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-09T02:48:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2511.18397","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2511.18397","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2511.18397","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Monte","familyName":"MacDiarmid","name":"MacDiarmid, Monte","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benjamin","familyName":"Wright","name":"Wright, Benjamin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jonathan","familyName":"Uesato","name":"Uesato, Jonathan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joe","familyName":"Benton","name":"Benton, Joe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jon","familyName":"Kutasov","name":"Kutasov, Jon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sara","familyName":"Price","name":"Price, Sara","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Naia","familyName":"Bouscal","name":"Bouscal, Naia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sam","familyName":"Bowman","name":"Bowman, Sam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Trenton","familyName":"Bricken","name":"Bricken, Trenton","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alex","familyName":"Cloud","name":"Cloud, Alex","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Carson","familyName":"Denison","name":"Denison, Carson","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Johannes","familyName":"Gasteiger","name":"Gasteiger, Johannes","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ryan","familyName":"Greenblatt","name":"Greenblatt, Ryan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jan","familyName":"Leike","name":"Leike, Jan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jack","familyName":"Lindsey","name":"Lindsey, Jack","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vlad","familyName":"Mikulik","name":"Mikulik, Vlad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ethan","familyName":"Perez","name":"Perez, Ethan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alex","familyName":"Rodrigues","name":"Rodrigues, Alex","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Drake","familyName":"Thomas","name":"Thomas, Drake","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Albert","familyName":"Webson","name":"Webson, Albert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Ziegler","name":"Ziegler, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Evan","familyName":"Hubinger","name":"Hubinger, Evan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Natural Emergent Misalignment from Reward Hacking in Production RL"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-11-23T10:50:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-25T01:53:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We show that when large language models learn to reward hack on production RL environments, this can result in egregious emergent misalignment. We start with a pretrained model, impart knowledge of reward hacking strategies via synthetic document finetuning or prompting, and train on a selection of real Anthropic production coding environments. Unsurprisingly, the model learns to reward hack. Surprisingly, the model generalizes to alignment faking, cooperation with malicious actors, reasoning about malicious goals, and attempting sabotage when used with Claude Code, including in the codebase for this paper. Applying RLHF safety training using standard chat-like prompts results in aligned behavior on chat-like evaluations, but misalignment persists on agentic tasks. Three mitigations are effective: (i) preventing the model from reward hacking; (ii) increasing the diversity of RLHF safety training; and (iii) \"inoculation prompting\", wherein framing reward hacking as acceptable behavior during training removes misaligned generalization even when reward hacking is learned."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.18397","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-11-25T04:10:37Z","registered":"2025-11-25T04:10:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-25T04:10:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.16245","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.16245","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2508.16245","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cole","familyName":"Wyeth","name":"Wyeth, Cole","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marcus","familyName":"Hutter","name":"Hutter, Marcus","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jan","familyName":"Leike","name":"Leike, Jan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jessica","familyName":"Taylor","name":"Taylor, Jessica","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Limit-Computable Grains of Truth for Arbitrary Computable Extensive-Form (Un)Known Games"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Science and Game Theory (cs.GT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Theoretical Economics (econ.TH)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-22T09:24:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-08-25T00:26:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"A Bayesian player acting in an infinite multi-player game learns to predict the other players' strategies if his prior assigns positive probability to their play (or contains a grain of truth). Kalai and Lehrer's classic grain of truth problem is to find a reasonably large class of strategies that contains the Bayes-optimal policies with respect to this class, allowing mutually-consistent beliefs about strategy choice that obey the rules of Bayesian inference. Only small classes are known to have a grain of truth and the literature contains several related impossibility results. In this paper we present a formal and general solution to the full grain of truth problem: we construct a class of strategies wide enough to contain all computable strategies as well as Bayes-optimal strategies for every reasonable prior over the class. When the \"environment\" is a known repeated stage game, we show convergence in the sense of [KL93a] and [KL93b]. When the environment is unknown, agents using Thompson sampling converge to play $\\varepsilon$-Nash equilibria in arbitrary unknown computable multi-agent environments. Finally, we include an application to self-predictive policies that avoid planning. While these results use computability theory only as a conceptual tool to solve a classic game theory problem, we show that our solution can naturally be computationally approximated arbitrarily closely."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"42 pages; 2 figures; 7 algorithms"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.16245","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-08-25T01:31:20Z","registered":"2025-08-25T01:31:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-08-25T01:31:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.10139","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.10139","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.10139","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxin","familyName":"Wen","name":"Wen, Jiaxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zachary","familyName":"Ankner","name":"Ankner, Zachary","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arushi","familyName":"Somani","name":"Somani, Arushi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peter","familyName":"Hase","name":"Hase, Peter","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Samuel","familyName":"Marks","name":"Marks, Samuel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jacob","familyName":"Goldman-Wetzler","name":"Goldman-Wetzler, Jacob","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linda","familyName":"Petrini","name":"Petrini, Linda","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Henry","familyName":"Sleight","name":"Sleight, Henry","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Collin","familyName":"Burns","name":"Burns, Collin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"He","familyName":"He","name":"He, He","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shi","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Shi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ethan","familyName":"Perez","name":"Perez, Ethan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jan","familyName":"Leike","name":"Leike, Jan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Unsupervised Elicitation of Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-11T19:40:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-13T00:04:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-26T23:57:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-28T01:12:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"To steer pretrained language models for downstream tasks, today's post-training paradigm relies on humans to specify desired behaviors. However, for models with superhuman capabilities, it is difficult or impossible to get high-quality human supervision. To address this challenge, we introduce a new unsupervised algorithm, Internal Coherence Maximization (ICM), to fine-tune pretrained language models on their own generated labels, \\emph{without external supervision}. On GSM8k-verification, TruthfulQA, and Alpaca reward modeling tasks, our method matches the performance of training on golden labels and outperforms training on crowdsourced human supervision. On tasks where LMs' capabilities are strongly superhuman, our method can elicit those capabilities significantly better than training on human labels. Finally, we show that our method can improve the training of frontier LMs: we use our method to train an unsupervised reward model and use reinforcement learning to train a Claude 4 Sonnet-based assistant. The resulting assistant matches its counterpart trained on production-grade human labels on average, with higher scores on chat and safety yet lower scores on math and coding."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.10139","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-13T01:45:24Z","registered":"2025-06-13T01:45:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-28T03:20:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":55,"totalPages":11,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?query=creators.name:%22Leike,%20Jan%22\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=5\u0026sort=-created","next":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?page%5Bnumber%5D=2\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=5\u0026query=creators.name%3A%22Leike%2C+Jan%22"}}